      <tei:div type="colophon" subtype="notes">
        <tei:p>“Heil’ge Sabbath-Ruhe” is a hymn selected by Rabbi Gotthold Salomon, Immanuel Wohlwill, and Maimon Fraenkel for inclusion in the <tei:hi rend="italic">Hamburg Temple Hymnal</tei:hi> (1833), hymn No342, p. 415. The first, fourth, and sixth stanzas were translated by Rabbi James Koppel Gutheim and published as “Der Sabbath (The Sabbath)” in his <tei:hi rend="italic">Hymns, for Divine Service in the Temple Emanu-El</tei:hi> (1871) as hymn No1, pp. 2-3. The hymn also appears as the preface to Gotthold Salomon’s sermon “Der Segen des Sabbathtages” in <tei:hi rend="italic">Der berg des Herrn: Kanzel-vorträge über den Decalog</tei:hi> (1846), p. 32 lending me to think that the hymn was at the very least appreciated by him, and possibly also written by him. –Aharon Varady</tei:p>
      </tei:div>
      <j:endConditional target="#osp_51216_notes"/>
